# Solving the PDE by Iteration

Let $`L` be an invertible linear elliptic operator. Writing

$$`
Lu+N(u)=f
`

as

$$`
u=T(u),\qquad T(u)=L^{-1}(f-N(u)),
`

turns the PDE into an iteration problem. Starting from $`u_0`, define
$`u_{k+1}=T(u_k)`. If $`T` contracts distances by a factor $`q<1`, the
sequence converges to the unique fixed point and

$$`
\lVert u-u_k\rVert
\leq\frac{q^k}{1-q}\lVert u_1-u_0\rVert.
`

This a priori estimate bounds the error using the first increment and the
iteration count. Uniqueness follows directly from contraction: two fixed
points have distance at most $`q` times that same distance. The theorem of
{Informal.citet "banach-1922"}[] is this metric argument in general form.

The denominator $`1-q` comes from summing the remaining increments:
$`\|u_{k+j+1}-u_{k+j}\|\leq q^j\|u_{k+1}-u_k\|`.
It also gives an estimate for any candidate $`v`, whether or not it was
produced by iteration. Since $`u=T(u)`,

$$`
\|u-v\|
\leq\|T(u)-T(v)\|+\|T(v)-v\|
\leq q\|u-v\|+\|T(v)-v\|,
`

and hence

$$`
\|u-v\|\leq\frac{\|T(v)-v\|}{1-q}.
`

This estimate connects an approximate solution to the existence theorem: the
computable defect $`\|T(v)-v\|` is useful because contraction controls how
much it can be amplified.

This method is especially useful when the operator is not generated by a
symmetric energy. For example, a first-order term in
$`-\Delta u+b(x)\cdot\nabla u+g(u)=f` may destroy the symmetry needed for a
variational formulation while leaving the linear inverse bounded. Even when an
energy exists, the fixed-point formulation can reveal quantitative dependence
on the data more directly.

The contraction constant exposes the limitation. If the inverse has norm
$`\lVert L^{-1}\rVert` and $`N` is Lipschitz with constant $`K`, then

$$`
\operatorname{Lip}(T)\leq\lVert L^{-1}\rVert K.
`

A product below one is a sufficient contraction condition. For a coercive
operator with modulus $`c>0`, the bound
$`\lVert L^{-1}\rVert\leq c^{-1}` gives the sufficient condition $`K<c`.
A cubic nonlinearity is not globally Lipschitz, so it falls outside this
global contraction regime even though its quartic potential may fit the
variational method.

:::group "methods.semilinear_nonvariational"
Semilinear fixed-point methods
:::

:::definition "methods.semilinear.picard_map" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.Weak.semilinearPicardMap, SobolevSpace.Weak.semilinearPicardSolution") (uses := "elliptic.lax_milgram")
Let $`B` be a bounded coercive bilinear form on a real Hilbert space $`H`,
let $`N:H\to H^*` be a nonlinear load, and let $`\ell\in H^*`. Writing
$`S_B:H^*\to H` for the Lax–Milgram solution operator of $`B`, the Picard
map freezes the nonlinearity and solves the remaining linear problem:

$$`
T(u)=S_B\bigl(\ell-N(u)\bigr),
\qquad\text{so that}\qquad
B(T(u),v)=\ell(v)-N(u)[v]\quad(v\in H).
`

When $`N` is Lipschitz with constant $`K` and $`\lVert S_B\rVert K<1`, the
Picard solution is the fixed point of $`T` supplied by Banach's theorem.
The contraction hypothesis is an explicit argument of the Picard solution.
:::

The contraction estimate and the identification of fixed points with weak
solutions are independent facts; the second needs no smallness condition.

:::theorem "methods.semilinear.picard_contraction" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.Weak.semilinearPicardMap_contractingWith, SobolevSpace.Weak.semilinearPicardSolution_spec, SobolevSpace.Weak.isFixedPt_semilinearPicardMap_iff") (uses := "methods.semilinear.picard_map")
