If $`N` is Lipschitz with constant $`K` and $`q=\lVert S_B\rVert K<1`, then
the Picard map is a contraction with constant $`q`:

$$`
\lVert T(u)-T(w)\rVert\leq q\,\lVert u-w\rVert\qquad(u,w\in H).
`

A field $`u` is a fixed point of $`T` if and only if it solves the semilinear
weak equation

$$`
B(u,v)+N(u)[v]=\ell(v)\qquad(v\in H),
`

and in particular the Picard solution satisfies this equation for every test
field $`v`.
:::

:::proof "methods.semilinear.picard_contraction"
The map $`u\mapsto\ell-N(u)` is $`K`-Lipschitz into $`H^*`, and the bounded
linear operator $`S_B` multiplies Lipschitz constants by at most
$`\lVert S_B\rVert`, so $`T` is $`q`-Lipschitz; the hypothesis $`q<1` makes
it a contraction. If $`T(u)=u`, then $`u` is the Lax–Milgram solution for the
load $`\ell-N(u)`, so $`B(u,v)=\ell(v)-N(u)[v]` for every $`v`. Conversely,
that identity says $`u` is a weak solution for the load $`\ell-N(u)`, and
uniqueness of Lax–Milgram solutions gives $`T(u)=u`. The Picard solution is
the fixed point of the contraction, so it satisfies the weak equation.
:::

Because every weak solution is a fixed point of $`T`, uniqueness of the
Banach fixed point is uniqueness of the weak solution.

:::theorem "methods.semilinear.contraction" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.Weak.existsUnique_semilinear_of_small_lipschitz")
