%%%
A coercive variational operator plus a globally Lipschitz nonlinear dual load
has a unique solution when the norm of the Lax–Milgram solution operator
times the Lipschitz constant is strictly less than one. If $`S_B:H^*\to H`
is the linear solution operator and $`N:H\to H^*` has Lipschitz constant
$`K`, then the Picard map

$$`
T(u)=S_B(\ell-N(u)),\qquad
\operatorname{Lip}(T)\leq\lVert S_B\rVert K<1,
`

has a unique fixed point.
:::

:::proof "methods.semilinear.contraction" (uses := "methods.semilinear.picard_contraction")
Lax–Milgram supplies the bounded solution operator $`S_B`. For two fields,
linearity of $`S_B` and Lipschitz continuity of $`N` give
$`\|T(u)-T(v)\|\leq\|S_B\|K\|u-v\|`. The assumed product is below one, so
$`T` is a strict contraction on the complete Hilbert space. Banach's fixed
point theorem gives a unique $`u=T(u)`, and expanding that identity yields
the semilinear weak equation.
:::

For the semilinear weak problem, define $`A_Bv=B(v,\mathbin\cdot)` and the
dual-space residual $`r(v)=A_Bv+N(v)-\ell`. Since $`S_BA_Bv=v`,

$$`
T(v)-v=-S_Br(v),\qquad
\|u-v\|_H
\leq\frac{\|S_B\|}{1-q}\,\|r(v)\|_{H^*},
\quad q=\|S_B\|K<1.
`

This derived estimate identifies what a CAS certificate would need to
supply: a bound in the dual norm appearing here. A pointwise residual
enclosure can contribute to that bound, but passing to the dual norm needs
integrability and the relevant test-function estimates. The candidate must
also belong to the Hilbert space encoding the boundary condition. A small
number returned for a sampled residual does not supply these hypotheses.

:::theorem "methods.semilinear.contraction_coercivity" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.Weak.existsUnique_semilinear_of_lipschitz_lt_coercivity")
