Let $`f\in L^2(\Omega)` and suppose the Dirichlet form on $`H^1_0(\Omega)`
satisfies $`B(v,v)\geq c\|v\|_{H^1}^2` for every $`v`, with $`c>0`.
Let $`g:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R` satisfy $`g(0)=0` and
$`|g(a)-g(b)|\leq K|a-b|` for all $`a,b`, where $`0\leq K<c`. Then
there is a unique $`u\in H^1_0(\Omega)` satisfying

$$`
B(u,v)+\int_\Omega g(u)v\,dx=\int_\Omega fv\,dx
\qquad(v\in H^1_0(\Omega))
`

The norm is the full $`H^1` graph norm, so the coercivity hypothesis includes
control of the $`L^2` coordinate. This theorem gives a small-Lipschitz
Banach-contraction regime.
:::

:::proof "methods.semilinear.dirichlet_contraction" (uses := "methods.semilinear.contraction_coercivity, methods.dirichlet.nemytskii_bridge")
Since $`g(0)=0`, composition by $`g` maps $`L^2(\Omega)` to itself with
Lipschitz constant $`K`. The base-coordinate map from $`H^1_0` to $`L^2`
and the map sending $`h\in L^2` to the load $`v\mapsto\int_\Omega hv`
both have norm at most one. Thus the nonlinear load has Lipschitz constant
at most $`K`, and $`K<c` gives existence and uniqueness by the abstract
contraction theorem.
:::

# Compactness and Monotonicity Beyond Contractions

Schauder's fixed-point theorem
{Informal.citet "schauder-1930"}[] replaces metric contraction by continuity,
compactness, and invariance of a convex set. It can treat maps with no
small-Lipschitz estimate, though it does not generally give uniqueness or a
convergent Picard iteration. Minty and Browder developed another direction:
monotonicity and coercivity of a nonlinear operator can replace compactness in
existence arguments {Informal.citet "minty-1962"}[]
{Informal.citet "browder-1963"}[]. Schauder's theorem and Schaefer's theorem
are proved for compact maps on real normed spaces. The validation argument below
uses Schauder in a weaker topology to obtain existence near an approximate solution.

## From an approximate solution to an exact one

A computed candidate need not solve the equation exactly. The question is whether its
residual is small enough, relative to the inverse of the linearized operator, to place
an exact solution nearby. Hou, Wang, and Yang develop this argument first for a
nonlinear Poisson equation, then for their fluid profiles
{Informal.citet "hou-wang-yang-2025"}[§§3.1–3.2]. We formalize the residual-correction
identity and the weak-topology existence argument generally, and specialize them to
Poisson's equation. The Navier–Stokes nonuniqueness theorem built on them is
formalized in the companion repository [BlowupZoo](https://github.com/Robertboy18/BlowupZoo).

Suppose $`A=L_0+K`, with $`L_0` coercive. Let $`S` extract coordinates,
$`\Psi S` interpolate, and $`\Phi` contain approximate solves of
$`A\Phi=-K\Psi`. Their residual is $`A\Phi+K\Psi`. Keeping this residual gives an
exact identity rather than treating the approximate inverse as exact.

:::theorem "methods.validated.finite_rank_correction" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "PDE.Analysis.Operator.FiniteRankCorrection.corrected_eq_comp, PDE.Analysis.Operator.FiniteRankCorrection.corrected_pairing_ge")
%%%
source := {
  document := "hou-wang-yang-2025"
  spans := #[{
    citation := "Sections 3.1.2–3.2.1"
    text := some { path := "Blueprint/Sources/NavierStokesNonuniqueness.md", startLine := 9, endLine := 16 }
  }]
}
%%%
For bounded weak-form operators and coordinate maps,

$$`
\begin{aligned}
B&=L_0+K(I-\Psi S)\\
 &\quad +(A\Phi+K\Psi)S\\
 &=A(I+\Phi S).
\end{aligned}
`

If $`L_0[u,u]\ge c_0\lVert u\rVert^2`, the interpolation tail $`K(I-\Psi S)`
has norm at most $`\delta`, and the residual after applying the coordinate map,
$`(A\Phi+K\Psi)S`, has norm at most $`\eta`, then

$$`B[u,u]\ge(c_0-\delta-\eta)\lVert u\rVert^2.`

The identities hold for any coordinate space; finite dimension is needed by a numerical
construction, rather than by this algebraic transfer.
:::

:::proof "methods.validated.finite_rank_correction"
Expand the interpolation tail and the complete solve residual. The two
terms $`K\Psi S` cancel, leaving $`A+A\Phi S`. The operator norm of
$`B-L_0` is at most $`\delta+\eta`; evaluating this difference on $`u`
and then pairing with $`u` costs at most
$`(\delta+\eta)\lVert u\rVert^2`. Subtract this bound from the
coercivity estimate for $`L_0`.
:::

For $`-\Delta u+u^2=f`, write $`u=p+w`, where $`p` is the candidate. Its residual load
is $`E=f-(-\Delta p+p^2)`, and the correction equation is

$$`(-\Delta+2p)w+w^2=E.`

Let $`R` be a bounded reconstruction with $`B=(-\Delta+2p)R` coercive with modulus
$`c>0`. Solve for $`x` with $`w=Rx`. Lax–Milgram defines the iteration
$`x\mapsto B^{-1}(E-(Rx)^2)`. If $`\lVert R\rVert\le g` and the
$`H^1_0\to L^4` inclusion has norm at most $`s`, then
$`\lVert(Rx)^2\rVert_{(H^1_0)^*}\le s^2g^2\lVert x\rVert^2`.
Thus a residual bound $`\lVert E\rVert\le\beta` and the scalar inequality

$$`\beta+s^2g^2r^2\le cr`

make the energy ball of radius $`r` invariant.

:::theorem "methods.validated.quadratic_dirichlet" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.exists_dirichlet_quadratic_weak_solution_near") (uses := "elliptic.lax_milgram, methods.validated.finite_rank_correction")
%%%
source := {
  document := "hou-wang-yang-2025"
  spans := #[{
    citation := "Section 3.1"
    text := some { path := "Blueprint/Sources/NavierStokesNonuniqueness.md", startLine := 9, endLine := 16 }
  }]
}
%%%
Let $`\Omega` be bounded and open in dimension $`1\le n\le3`, and let
$`p\in H^1_0(\Omega)`, $`f\in L^2(\Omega)`. Assume the linear reconstruction,
coercivity, embedding, and residual bounds above, together with
$`\lVert eRu\rVert_{L^4}\le\gamma\lVert eu\rVert_{L^4}` for some finite
$`\gamma`, where $`e:H^1_0\to L^4` is the canonical inclusion.
If $`r\ge0` and the radius budget holds, there is a weak solution $`u` with

$$`\lVert u-p\rVert_{H^1_0}\le gr.`

For every $`v\in H^1_0(\Omega)`,

$$`\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v+\int_\Omega u^2v=\int_\Omega fv.`

The nonlinear pairing is integrable for every test. Existence is asserted; uniqueness
and convergence of the iteration are not conclusions of this theorem.
:::

:::proof "methods.validated.quadratic_dirichlet"
Rellich compactness makes the image of the closed energy ball compact in $`L^4`.
Hölder's inequality bounds the quadratic load and its difference there. The reconstruction
bound gives continuity of the iteration in this weaker topology, so Schauder supplies a
fixed point. The exact linear factorization and quadratic expansion recover the original
weak equation after reconstruction.
:::

The profile, coordinate space, and constants are inputs to the proof. Improving a bound
means rechecking the corresponding estimate and the scalar budget. Changing the equation
or approximation may require new analytic estimates. A successful scalar check alone
never certifies the PDE solution.

### A complete certificate on the unit interval

Take $`\Omega=(0,1)` and fix the forcing at $`f=1`. The linear Poisson solution

$$`p(x)=\frac{x(1-x)}2,\qquad -p''=1,\qquad p(0)=p(1)=0`

does not solve the quadratic equation: its residual is $`-p^2`. Its certified
range is $`0\le p\le1/8`, so the residual has dual norm at most $`1/64`.
For every zero-boundary test $`v`, the fundamental theorem of calculus and
Hölder give $`\lVert v\rVert_{L^4}\le\lVert v'\rVert_{L^2}`. Poincaré gives

$$`\begin{aligned}
\lVert v\rVert_{H^1}^2
&=\lVert v\rVert_{L^2}^2+\lVert v'\rVert_{L^2}^2\\
&\le2\lVert v'\rVert_{L^2}^2.
\end{aligned}`

Since $`p\ge0`, the linearized pairing adds the nonnegative term
$`2\int_0^1pv^2`. Thus identity reconstruction has $`g=\gamma=1`, the
embedding bound is $`s=1`, and the coercivity constant is $`c=1/2`.
The radius $`r=1/16` closes the budget:

$$`\frac1{64}+\frac1{256}=\frac5{256}\le\frac8{256}
=\frac12\cdot\frac1{16}.`

```Blueprint.leanExample
example : (1 : ℝ) / 64 + (1 / 16) ^ 2 ≤
    (1 / 2) * (1 / 16) := by
  cega
```

:::theorem "methods.validated.interval_poisson" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.exists_quadratic_dirichlet_solution_unit_interval, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.exists_dirichlet_quadratic_weak_solution_near_poisson, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.dirichlet_quadraticLinearization_self_nonneg, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.norm_dirichlet_quadraticLoad_le_of_ae_bound, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.norm_h10LpEmbedding_apply_le_one, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.norm_h10LpEmbedding_le_one, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.dirichletForm_coercive_bound_one") (uses := "methods.validated.quadratic_dirichlet")
There is $`u\in H^1_0(0,1)` such that

$$`\lVert u-p\rVert_{H^1}\le\frac1{16}.`

For every $`v\in H^1_0(0,1)`,

$$`\int_0^1u'v'+\int_0^1u^2v=\int_0^1v.`

The nonlinear pairing is integrable for every test. This is a complete interval
certificate using the validation argument of Hou–Wang–Yang
{Informal.citet "hou-wang-yang-2025"}[§3.1]; their example uses the
three-dimensional cube. The next application treats that domain directly.
:::

:::proof "methods.validated.interval_poisson"
The classical boundary-value theorem places the polynomial candidate in
$`H^1_0`. CAS proves its first and second derivatives; integration by parts
identifies its linear Poisson load. CEGA certifies the polynomial range and
the radius budget. The quantitative embedding, coercivity, and residual
estimates then supply every hypothesis of the validated existence theorem.
:::

## A validated solution on the unit cube

Let $`\Omega=(0,1)^3`, put $`q_i=x_i(1-x_i)`, and choose

$$`P=q_1q_2q_3,\qquad f=2(q_1q_2+q_1q_3+q_2q_3).`

CAS identifies $`-\Delta P=f`. The candidate has
$`0\leq P\leq1/64`, and its residual for the nonlinear equation is
$`f-(-\Delta P+P^2)=-P^2`. Clamping the coordinate quadratics outside
$`[0,1]` supplies a compact Lipschitz extension with zero boundary values,
so the candidate belongs to $`H^1_0(\Omega)` despite the cube's corners.

:::theorem "methods.validated.cube_poisson" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.UnitCube.exists_quadratic_dirichlet_solution, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.UnitCube.exists_weak_poisson_candidate, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.UnitCube.candidate_quadratic_residual, SobolevSpace.GraphSpace.UnitCube.volume_unitCube") (uses := "methods.validated.quadratic_dirichlet, sobolev.poincare_slab, sobolev.h10_explicit_critical")
There is $`u\in H^1_0(\Omega)` satisfying

$$`\|u-P\|_{H^1}\leq\frac1{512}.`

For every $`v\in H^1_0(\Omega)`,

$$`\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v+\int_\Omega u^2v=\int_\Omega fv.`

The forcing is the fixed linear Poisson forcing above. The construction
proves the weak Poisson identity, the candidate range, the domain volume,
the embedding bound and coercivity before applying validated existence.
:::

:::proof "methods.validated.cube_poisson"
Interior locality identifies the clamped extension's derivatives with those
of the polynomial. Weak integration by parts then proves the candidate's
Poisson identity against every $`H^1_0` test. The containing unit-width slab
gives Dirichlet coercivity $`1/2`; the explicit Sobolev estimate gives
$`\|v\|_{L^4}\leq12\|v\|_{H^1}` because the cube has volume one.
Nonnegativity of $`P` preserves coercivity under linearization.

The quadratic residual has dual norm at most $`1/4096`. With identity
reconstruction and radius $`r=1/512`, CEGA checks

$$`\frac1{4096}+144\left(\frac1{512}\right)^2
=\frac{13}{16384}\leq\frac{16}{16384}=\frac12\cdot\frac1{512}.`

Compactness of the zero-boundary $`L^4` inclusion supplies the fixed point.
This validates a concrete instance of the cube model in
{Informal.citet "hou-wang-yang-2025"}[§3.1].
:::

One theorem in the monotone direction still rests on a contraction.

:::theorem "methods.semilinear.monotone_lipschitz_bijective" (parent := "methods.semilinear_nonvariational") (lean := "SobolevSpace.Weak.bijective_semilinear_operator_of_semimonotone_of_lipschitzWith, SobolevSpace.Weak.bijective_semilinear_operator_of_monotone_of_lipschitzWith, SobolevSpace.Weak.existsUnique_semilinear_solution_of_semimonotone_of_lipschitzWith, SobolevSpace.Weak.existsUnique_semilinear_solution_of_monotone_of_lipschitzWith") (uses := "methods.semilinear.monotone_stability")
Let $`H` be a real Hilbert space, $`B` a bounded bilinear form with
$`c\lVert z\rVert^2\leq B(z,z)` for all $`z`, and $`N:H\to H^*` globally
Lipschitz. Suppose $`N` is semimonotone with loss $`L<c`:

$$`
-L\lVert u-w\rVert^2\leq\bigl(N(u)-N(w)\bigr)[u-w]\qquad(u,w\in H).
`

Then the semilinear operator $`u\mapsto B(u,\cdot)+N(u)` is a bijection of
$`H` onto $`H^*`; equivalently, for every $`\ell\in H^*`,

$$`
\exists!\,u\in H,\qquad B(u,v)+N(u)[v]=\ell(v)\quad(v\in H).
`

Monotone $`N` with $`c>0` is the case $`L=0`. The Lipschitz constant of
$`N` may be arbitrarily large. The proof is a forward-step contraction in
Hilbert space rather than an application of the Browder–Minty theorem.
:::

:::proof "methods.semilinear.monotone_lipschitz_bijective" (uses := "methods.semilinear.monotone_stability")
Coercivity of $`B` and semimonotonicity of $`N` make
$`A(u)=B(u,\cdot)+N(u)` strongly monotone with modulus $`m=c-L>0`, that is
$`m\lVert u-w\rVert^2\leq(A(u)-A(w))[u-w]`. Injectivity follows as in the
monotone stability estimate. For surjectivity, identify $`H^*` with $`H`
through the Riesz map and, for a target $`b`, consider the forward step
$`F_\tau(u)=u-\tau\bigl(A(u)-b\bigr)`. Expanding the square and using strong
monotonicity together with the Lipschitz constant $`K` of $`A` gives
$`\lVert F_\tau(u)-F_\tau(w)\rVert^2\leq(1-2\tau m+\tau^2K^2)\lVert u-w\rVert^2`,
so $`F_\tau` is a contraction for small $`\tau>0`. Its Banach fixed point
satisfies $`A(u)=b`. The unique-solvability form restates bijectivity load
by load.
:::

The library proves Browder–Minty surjectivity for coercive
hemicontinuous monotone maps from a reflexive real normed space to its
continuous dual. On a bounded domain of positive dimension it solves the
Dirichlet obstacle problem for an $`L^2` obstacle admitting an $`H^1_0`
majorant and an $`L^2` load; for a classical $`C^2` obstacle with
nonpositive boundary values on a $`C^2` graph domain,
{bpref "methods.constraints.obstacle_regularity"}[] upgrades a supplied
minimizer to $`H^2` and identifies its reaction and contact equations.
Proper lower-semicontinuous convex energies on a Hilbert space generate the
nonlinear contraction semiflow of
{bpref "evolution.semigroup.subgradient_closure"}[], and
{bpref "parabolic.gradient_diffusion"}[] applies subgradient flow to
uniformly convex gradient energies with bounded Hessian, a coefficient regime
that excludes the general $`p`-Laplacian. The library also proves the
mountain-pass theorem for $`C^1` Palais–Smale functionals with locally
Lipschitz gradient on a Hilbert space, and it gives a nonzero weak
solution of $`-\Delta u=f(u)` on a nonempty bounded open domain for a
superlinear subcritical $`f` (Evans §8.5.2, Theorem 3).

PDE-specific regularity is a second, independent step. The fixed-point theorem
constructs a weak solution in the energy space; upgrading it to a strong or
classical solution requires the elliptic estimates and nonlinear mapping
properties appropriate to the equation.
